      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Objective",
          "content": "We aimed to improve understanding of young adults' perceptions and interpretations of the contexts surrounding use."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Methods",
          "content": "We conducted focus groups with young adult hookah users (ages 18-28) to examine hookah-use experiences and risk perceptions."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Results",
          "content": "Two dominant themes that emerged from the discussions were the social aspects of use (eg, \"ending up\" at a hookah café while out with friends), and the mental and physiological reactions to use (eg, relaxation and a \"high\" feeling). Participants often argued that because they only used hookah every few weeks, hookah use was much less dangerous than cigarette use."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Conclusions",
          "content": "Findings suggest that hookah research should account for heuristic processing and point to several areas where better health communication and stricter regulatory policies are needed."
        }
      ]},
